    {
      "id": "d8-gtXfefk0Div0W8fazw",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 157.60056673668532,
      "y": -921.5625720637407,
      "width": 1216.0992431640625,
      "height": 1475,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a4",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1018026016,
      "version": 2199,
      "versionNonce": 1542575029,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1786539553593,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Functional Requirements:\n    - Create short url from long url\n    - Redirect to original url using short url\n    - User prefered alias?\n\nNon-functional requirements\n    - Low latency\n    - Uniqueness\n    - Scale to handle 100M DAU and 1B urls\n    - Highly available\n    - Eventual Consistency (some delay is acceptable \n        if short url doesn't work just after creation)\n      \nNote: Highly Consistency means strong read after write \n    means changes done at any point of time should reflect on immediate read.\nWhereas, in eventual consistency slight delay is acceptable after write.\n\n\nBack of the envelope estimation\n    Do the estimation while doing high level design. This way we can decide the design\n    base on BOEE. Like if user load is less, why need complicated architecture.\n\nCore Entities: As this is the very basic system design, we can have database design upfront. But in complex system design, \n    we keep changing the database design as per our need while HLD.\n    \n    user\n        - user_id\n        - user_name\n        - user_email\n\n    url_mapping\n        - short_url/alias?: primary key\n        - original_url\n        - expiration_time\n        - user_id: foreign key to user table\n\nAPIs\n        // Shorten the long url\n        POST  /shorten -> return short url\n                  \n                    Request body:\n                        {\n                            original_url,\n                            user_id,\n                            alias?,\n                            expiration_time?\n                        }\n\n                   Response body:\n                        {\n                            \"short_url\": \"\"\n                        }\n        \n        // Redirect to original url from short url\n        GET   /{short_url} -> redirect\n                \n                302 - redirected: this http status code tells browser to use returned url and redirect the user.\n                301 - redirected and browser cache short_url for future redirect.\n    ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Functional Requirements:\n    - Create short url from long url\n    - Redirect to original url using short url\n    - User prefered alias?\n\nNon-functional requirements\n    - Low latency\n    - Uniqueness\n    - Scale to handle 100M DAU and 1B urls\n    - Highly available\n    - Eventual Consistency (some delay is acceptable \n        if short url doesn't work just after creation)\n      \nNote: Highly Consistency means strong read after write \n    means changes done at any point of time should reflect on immediate read.\nWhereas, in eventual consistency slight delay is acceptable after write.\n\n\nBack of the envelope estimation\n    Do the estimation while doing high level design. This way we can decide the design\n    base on BOEE. Like if user load is less, why need complicated architecture.\n\nCore Entities: As this is the very basic system design, we can have database design upfront. But in complex system design, \n    we keep changing the database design as per our need while HLD.\n    \n    user\n        - user_id\n        - user_name\n        - user_email\n\n    url_mapping\n        - short_url/alias?: primary key\n        - original_url\n        - expiration_time\n        - user_id: foreign key to user table\n\nAPIs\n        // Shorten the long url\n        POST  /shorten -> return short url\n                  \n                    Request body:\n                        {\n                            original_url,\n                            user_id,\n                            alias?,\n                            expiration_time?\n                        }\n\n                   Response body:\n                        {\n                            \"short_url\": \"\"\n                        }\n        \n        // Redirect to original url from short url\n        GET   /{short_url} -> redirect\n                \n                302 - redirected: this http status code tells browser to use returned url and redirect the user.\n                301 - redirected and browser cache short_url for future redirect.\n    ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
